\date{2025-11-09T22:25:02Z}
\title{Typing rules for functions}

\import{swarm-macros}

\p{The typing rule for lambdas is straightforward:

\rules{
  \inferrule
    {\swty{\Gamma, x : \tau_1}{e}{\tau_2}{\Delta}}
    {\swty{\Gamma}{\lamt{x}{\tau_1}{e}}{\Fun{\tau_1}{\Delta}{\tau_2}}{\sf{Lambda}}}
}

Notice how the requirements for evaluating the body show up over the arrow in the result type, but are not needed to evaluate the lambda itself. We do not evaluate under lambdas, but defer evaluation of the body until evaluating an application to an argument.  However, evaluating a lambda does require the special \sf{Lambda} capability.
}

\p{Here is the typing rule for function application:

\rules{
  \inferrule
    {\swty{\Gamma}{f}{\Fun{\tau_1}{\Delta_1}{\tau_2}}{\Delta_2} \\
     \swty{\Gamma}{e}{\tau_1}{\Delta_3}
    }
    {\swty{\Gamma}{\app{f}{e}}{\tau_2}{\Delta_1 \cup \Delta_2 \cup \Delta_3 \cup \sf{Apply}}}
}

The capabilities needed to evaluate the application #{\app{f}{e}} are the union of all the capabilities needed to evaluate #{f}, to evaluate #{e}, and to evaluate the body of the function represented by #{f}, along with the special \sf{Apply} capability.

}